(3) Workforce. Against 2044, output is down 6% in 2045, 37% in 2050, 50% in 2052, 69% in 2055 and 100% in 2060. Give me the retention and transfer plan: Charleston, defence and a successor line [B-1941]. What does it take to keep a trained narrowbody workforce through to 2050, so that a launch in the last round could still be staffed?

(4) Suppliers and engines through the phase-out.
- Which suppliers become fragile as MAX volume falls, and which do we keep hot and funded [BX-1243, B-2330]?
- LEAP-1B OE and spares cover to 2060, and the -1B durability data CFM says it will report when the fleet data is in.
- Joint capacity planning, so CFM supports our tail and Airbus's lines [BX-1264, BX-1325].

(5) 787 Re-engine readiness, restated for the record (the Board's ask):
- the GEnx upgrade (ready since 2033, 17 years before a 2050 EIS) against your durability-first engine-path condition [BX-1298];
- strain alongside the Charleston expansion [BX-1317, B-2336];
- no production-certification concurrency [BX-1263];
- the watch item that it starts on GE alone.

(6) fps readiness, which I expect to hold:
- the 10-year ramp (EIS 2055) against the 7-year (EIS 2052);
- under code 7, the engine margin at EIS. GTF2 has been ready since 2036. An UltraFan NB selected now would be ready in 2052, but Rolls-Royce rules out a solo programme. The Joint Venture is unformed, because P&W has not committed. CFM is always available;
- durability for each named engine;
- your Renton wind-down condition, set against a MAX line that is already falling;
- two-front strain with the Re-engine: 2 years on the 10-year ramp; 5 years on the 7-year, which H4 bars;
- Solo or via Embraer on IP [BX-1330]: via Embraer runs $3.63-4.10B behind the Solo. Keep the Korean-supplier watch item.

(7) A share check. My reading of the lead rule (inference) is that no fps launched this round reaches 40% or 50% in a scored year, and that the long-run split would be about 35/65 (7-year) or 32/68 (10-year) from a launch now, and about 30/70 or 27/73 from a 2050 launch. Confirm or correct.

frame.initial_lean:
My lean is Do Nothing on every field, for the third time:
- fps hold (engine code null, fps_site new_site);
- rate_737 hold, the only value on offer;
- re787 hold.

The fps waits, because the business case isn't there. The best fps is $3.06B below Do Nothing in its best column. Every form fails Malave's buffer in S3, and it fails on its base case, not on the slip. At $3.06B or more it breaks the $2B cap in H8, and it buys no scored objective. Company doctrine agrees: in the late rounds, launch only if the fps beats Do Nothing by more than ε (profile §6). Airbus's timeline does not set our date [B-2313].

The 787 Re-engine is a tie this time, not a case. At +0.74 in three columns and -0.81 in S2 it is inside ε everywhere. Doing less, better [BX-1239] and the higher-confidence plan [BX-0548] both point to Do Nothing, as does company doctrine (no Re-engine below ε, profile §6 and §11.4). The Board's business-case test fails as well. So the hold stands on my own grounds, not only on the Board's. I would log the $0.74B as the Board's business-case threshold and claim no management premium.

I want the trade on the record plainly, because this is not a month's wait. Holding a third time means:
- the earliest EIS is 2057 or 2060, and the MAX line reaches zero in 2060;
- delivered share is 22.2% in 2050 and 12.7% in 2055;
- unless the 2050 case clears, this board has Boeing out of the narrowbody market.
I'm not going to launch an airplane to meet a date, and I'm not going to trade a hard rule for share. But we owe the Board, and ourselves, an honest view of whether 2050 can clear at all. That is why I ask Jay for the split.

What we do this round instead, at no grid cost:
- keep the fps definition, the production system and the workforce ready;
- keep fragile suppliers hot through the phase-out;
- keep the LEAP-1B supply and durability work going;
- restate the multi-source engine requirement.
When the case is there, I expect to bring the 10-year Solo, at Renton under Pope's condition, with code 7, no date before launch and headroom in the EIS.

Expected column: S1, rivals hold, with Low confidence on Airbus. S2 is more plausible now that NGSA is in service.

What would change my mind:
- a correction to my numbers from Jay;
- a readiness gap from Stephanie;
- a row from either of you that beats Do Nothing by more than ε and passes the buffer.

It is so much more important that we do this right than fast, and doing it right includes saying out loud what waiting costs.

